keel-overlay-anubis (0.1.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The policy of anubis@keel.service is /etc/anubis/keel-policy.yaml now,
    not upstream's default policy alone: the drop-in directory
    /etc/anubis/keel.d/*.yaml is imported first, and the default rules
    after it (handbook decision 0062, the Nextcloud appliance: a sync
    client carries no browser and no cookie). Anubis evaluates the bots
    list in order and the first rule that matches wins, so a drop-in of an
    appliance may allow what the default policy would challenge or deny.
    Anubis reads an imported file as a list of rules, not as a policy, so
    the default rules come from (data)/meta/default-config.yaml, and the
    other sections of upstream's default policy of 1.27.0 (thresholds,
    status codes, store, honeypot, Open Graph) are copied into the file.
  * The package ships /etc/anubis/keel.d/keel-placeholder.yaml: a glob that
    matches no file is fatal at the startup of Anubis, and a machine whose
    appliance ships no drop-in must still start. A drop-in is a list of
    rules, with no "bots:" key. The placeholder's regular expression
    matches nothing; it only makes the glob match a file.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

keel-overlay-anubis (0.1.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A WebSocket passes the Anubis front (Keel-Linux/common#58).
    /etc/nginx/snippets/keel-anubis.conf now sets the Upgrade and
    Connection headers of the request, through the map in the new file
    /etc/nginx/conf.d/keel-anubis.conf ($keel_anubis_connection: "upgrade"
    for an upgrade, "close" for any other request). Nginx dropped both
    headers before, so Anubis never handed the upgrade to the app and
    /websocket answered 400, for example the bus of Odoo. Anubis checks
    the cookie before it hands over a request, so a client with no pass
    still gets the challenge.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:00:00 +0000

keel-overlay-anubis (0.1.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First release (handbook decision 0041, first implementation, step 6).
    Depends on anubis 1.27.0, built by Keel (step 5); ships the overlay
    manifest as /usr/share/keel/overlays/anubis.yaml. Its unit is the
    anubis package's template instance, anubis@keel.service, and its port
    declares the address it binds, ::1, both departing from the worked
    example of docs/manifest-v1.md, which names anubis.service.
  * /etc/anubis/keel.env: Anubis on [::1]:8923 only, its metrics on a
    unix socket, handing what it allows back to Nginx on
    unix:/run/nginx/keel-app.sock (decision 0042); upstream's default
    policy, the one policy of version 1. /etc/nginx/snippets/
    keel-anubis.conf sends a location through it.
  * Installed disabled and stopped: nothing enables the instance. On its
    first start keel-overlay-anubis-key.service provides the signing key,
    /etc/anubis/keel.key, when it is not there, so no image carries one:
    a link to the file the instance spec names, nothing on a database
    replica or where keel.key.from-primary marks the key as the
    primary's, else a generated one. postrm stops and disables the
    instance and deletes the key on purge.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 03:00:00 +0000
